<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Ladwa State</h1><p class="st-meta">1763 CE – 1846 CE (approx.) · capital Ladwa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Sikh chiefship near Kurukshetra whose ruler sided with the Lahore army in the First Anglo-Sikh War and lost his state.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gurdit-singh-of-ladwa">Gurdit Singh</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> sardar, seized Ladwa and nearby territory around 1763 when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhs">Sikhs</a> overran the Mughal province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirhind">Sirhind</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Ladwa joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cis-sutlej-states">Cis-Sutlej states</a> under British protection in 1809. Its Raja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajit-singh-of-ladwa">Ajit Singh</a> was among the more substantial of the smaller chiefs, but kept close ties with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-sikh-war">First Anglo-Sikh War</a> of 1845–46 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajit-singh-of-ladwa">Ajit Singh</a> joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> army and took part in the campaign around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-baddowal">Buddowal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-aliwal">Aliwal</a>. The British confiscated Ladwa in 1846, and its lands became part of a British district, now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurukshetra">Kurukshetra</a> district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haryana">Haryana</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ladwa-state.jpg" alt="Ladwa State" width="320" height="379"><figcaption>Image: Cartographers and surveyors of the British East India Company · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Detail_of_the_main,_continuous_tract_of_territory_of_Ladwa_State_from_a_map_created_by_the_British_East_India_Company_of_the_Malwa_region_of_Punjab_showing_the_various_polities,_borders,_and_settlements_of_the_area,_ca.1829%E2%80%931835.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1763 CE – 1846 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ladwa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gurdit Singh, Ajit Singh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sikhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Punjabi, Haryanvi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ladwa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1804&amp;focus=ladwa-state" aria-label="Ladwa State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6682 1082 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ladwa State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7002 1338L7364 1601"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7364 1601h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7002 1338h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7002 1338h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Ladwa. <a href="/atlas?year=1804&amp;focus=ladwa-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

